  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry weather will continue through Sunday, with above \nnormal temperatures expected.\n\nSurface low over northern Quebec this morning will continue to \nprogress northeast into the Canadian Maritimes, pulling a weak cold \nfront across the Great Lakes. Meanwhile, surface high pressure \ncentered over the Mid-Atlantic will continue to ridge northward \nacross New York State today. Overall, dry weather will prevail \ntoday, along with slightly cooler high temperatures this afternoon \ndue to the cold frontal passage earlier in the day.\n\nAs the aforementioned surface high shifts across the Northeast/New \nEngland this weekend, winds will become more southerly which will \nact to limit any lake influences. Additionally, downsloping flow in \ncombination with 850mb temperatures peaking above the climatological \n90th percentile for mid-November, will support high temperatures \nSaturday afternoon a good 15 to 20 degrees above average. The warm \nweather will then persist  through Sunday ahead of the upper level \npattern shift beginning Sunday. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Next chance for precipitation and cooler air to \narrive late this weekend and last into early next week.\n\nThe mid to upper level pattern late this weekend and into the \nearliest portions of next week will feature a trough overspreading \nthe region from the north-central United States Sunday to the \neastern Great Lakes through Tuesday, where a ridge will then build \neast Tuesday night through Wednesday. Meanwhile at the surface, a \ncold front will sweep east across western and north-central New York \nSunday night, with surface high pressure gradually shifting east \nacross north-central United States and into the Great Lakes Monday \nthrough Wednesday.\n\nGetting more into the specifics, with the frontal passage Sunday \nnight into Monday morning, expect rain to overspread the region from \nwest to east due to low-level convergence and moist ascent along the \ncold front and moist isentropic lift ahead of the trough axis aloft. \nIn the wake of the frontal passage, a colder airmass will filter \nacross the region Monday. While a dry weather is expected to return, \nthe post-front airmass should be cold and moist enough to support \nlake-effect rain and/or snow showers southeast and south of the \neastern Great Lakes Monday into Tuesday. Any lake-effect \nprecipitation should end by Wednesday as a lowering subsidence \ninversion and continued low-level dry air advection cause lake-\ninduced instability to wane.",
